;; spec:   types §2.12 (not an ownership rule; method.md rule 6)
;; expect: accept
;; result: -9223372036854775807
;; audit:  clean
;; types §2.12: "Integer division and remainder trap on zero; signed
;; overflow wraps (Decided: it is what lIR emits and what the interpreter
;; can match exactly)". So (/ i64-min -1) wraps to i64-min and (rem
;; i64-min -1) is 0; the program adds 1: -9223372036854775807.
;;
;; Observed (fibref run):
;;   result: -9223372036854775807
;;   audit:  clean=true leak-cycles=0 leaks=0 errors=0
;; fibref follows the text. The text's justification is wrong: lIR maps
;; / and rem to LLVM sdiv/srem, for which i64-min / -1 is undefined
;; behaviour (on x86-64 the idiv instruction raises SIGFPE), not a wrap;
;; so a compiled program cannot "match exactly" without an explicit test.
;; Related gaps in the same section: shl/shr/sar by an amount >= the
;; width are not specified at all; fibref masks the amount ((shl 1 64) =
;; 1, (sar -1 65) = -1) where LLVM's shl/lshr/ashr give poison; and
;; (fptosi i64 x) of NaN or of an out-of-range float gives 0 or a
;; saturated value in fibref ((fptosi i64 (/ 1.0 0.0)) =
;; 9223372036854775807) where LLVM's fptosi gives poison.
;; Diagnosis: spec (types §2.12 must say what these operations do and
;; the compiled mapping must emit the tests that make it so).
(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((m (- (- 0 9223372036854775807) 1)))
    (+ (+ (/ m -1) (rem m -1)) 1)))
